            },
            "faq_accordion": {
              "type": "accordion",
              "settings": {
                "icon": "caret",
                "dividers": true,
                "type_preset": "h6",
                "inherit_color_scheme": true,
                "border": "none",
                "border_width": 1,
                "border_opacity": 100,
                "border_radius": 0,
                "padding-block-start": 0,
                "padding-block-end": 0,
                "padding-inline-start": 0,
                "padding-inline-end": 0
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_row_1": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "What muscles does the 360° Rotating Wrist & Forearm Trainer target?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_1": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>The 360° rotation targets your entire forearm — wrist flexors and extensors, pronators, supinators, and grip muscles. This full rotational range activates muscle groups that standard dumbbells and static grip trainers completely miss, giving you balanced forearm development and functional strength.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_1"]
                },
                "faq_row_2": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "Is it suitable for beginners or people new to wrist training?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_2": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>Absolutely. Start with just 2–3 minutes per hand daily and increase gradually. The resistance is controlled by your own movement speed and tension, so beginners can ease in gently while advanced users can push for a deep forearm burn. Most people notice increased grip endurance within the first two weeks.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_2"]
                },
                "faq_row_3": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "How is this different from a regular grip trainer or stress ball?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_3": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>Regular grip trainers only work finger flexion in one plane. The 360° Rotating Wrist Trainer adds full rotational resistance — pronation, supination, and radial/ulnar deviation — which builds the complete set of forearm muscles responsible for power, stability, and injury prevention in sport and daily life.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_3"]
                },
                "faq_row_4": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "Can I use it for wrist or elbow injury rehabilitation?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_4": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>Yes, the low-impact rotational motion makes it widely used in physical therapy for wrist sprains, tennis elbow, golfer's elbow, and post-fracture recovery. The smooth 360° movement promotes blood flow and tissue remodeling without the jarring impact of weights. Always consult your physiotherapist before starting a rehab program.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_4"]
                },
                "faq_row_5": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "How quickly will I see results?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_5": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>Based on customer feedback:<br>Week 1–2: You'll feel the forearm engagement immediately and notice reduced wrist fatigue at your desk<br>Week 3–4: Grip strength noticeably improves during workouts, climbing, or racket sports<br>Week 6–8: Visible forearm development and sustained improvements in functional grip endurance<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_5"]
                },
                "faq_row_6": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "Can athletes like rock climbers, golfers, or tennis players benefit?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_6": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>Absolutely. Rock climbers use it to build finger and forearm endurance. Golfers and tennis players use it to strengthen the rotational muscles responsible for swing power and prevent lateral epicondylitis. Martial artists and BJJ practitioners use it for clinch and grip dominance. Any sport requiring wrist control benefits directly.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_6"]
                },
                "faq_row_7": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "Is it safe if I have carpal tunnel syndrome or joint issues?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_7": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>The smooth rotational motion is generally well-tolerated by people with mild carpal tunnel symptoms, as it promotes circulation without compression. However, everyone's condition is different. Always consult your doctor or physiotherapist before starting any exercise program if you have a diagnosed joint or nerve condition.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_7"]
                },
                "faq_row_8": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "Can I use it at my desk or office?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_8": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>Yes — this is one of its biggest advantages. The compact size fits in a drawer or bag. You can use it silently while on a call, watching a video, or during any break. Many desk workers use it to combat repetitive strain from typing and reduce afternoon wrist fatigue without leaving their seat.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_8"]
                },
                "faq_row_9": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "How many minutes per day should I use it?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_9": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>Start with 3–5 minutes per hand, once daily. As your endurance builds, progress to 2–3 sets of 5 minutes per hand. Advanced users train up to 20 minutes daily. Consistency matters more than duration — daily short sessions outperform occasional long ones for forearm development.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_9"]
                },
                "faq_row_10": {
                  "type": "_accordion-row",
                  "settings": {
                    "heading": "Can I return it if it's not for me?",
                    "open_by_default": false,
                    "icon": "question_mark",
                    "width": 20
                  },
                  "blocks": {
                    "faq_text_10": {
                      "type": "text",
                      "settings": { "text": "<p>Absolutely! We offer a 30-day money-back guarantee. Try it risk-free. If you don't feel a difference in your grip and forearm strength within 30 days, return it for a full refund — no questions asked.<\/p>", "width": "100%" }
                    }
                  },
                  "block_order": ["faq_text_10"]
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_row_1","faq_row_2","faq_row_3","faq_row_4","faq_row_5",
                "faq_row_6","faq_row_7","faq_row_8","faq_row_9","faq_row_10"
              ]
            }
          },
